  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain moves in tonight, heavy at times into\nSunday. \n\nSome dry air at low levels, most of the radar returns this\nmorning so far have been at mid levels, but added slight \nchance POPS far western PA to match others, as some rain \nreaching the ground across the far west as of early aft.\n\nHigh pressure over Quebec will give way to a fast moving \nlow from the central states moving eastward across PA on\nSunday. Trends are for the low to be a bit further south\nand faster now. Expect steady rain to move into western\nareas later this evening. Bulk of the activity is over \nby early evening. A few showers could linger into Monday.\n\nThe airmass is rather cool and dry to start. However, still\nhave to watch the far south for thunder, given fcst temps. \nThunder could be a bit further north, but mainly elevated\nin that case. \n\nIt has been wet, but with the trend with lower QPF and\na bit faster movement, not really expecting hydro concerns\nat this pt. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Rainy pattern continues into much of next week,\nalthough uncertainty remains in the details. On the cool side \nas well.\n\nA large high over Quebec will push southward on Monday, giving\nthe area the best chance for dry conditions. Monday looking \nlike the best day to be outside.\n\nAs one heads into Tuesday and Wednesday, a wedge of colder\nand more stable air will be over the area, given this CAD\npattern. At this time, the highest POPS are over the west\nand south, given an inverted trough. \n\nPerhaps some improvement later Thursday, as a coastal low\nramps up off the southeast coast. \n\nWhile low temperatures will be not that far from normal,\nhigh temperatures will be colder than normal.",
